WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.120
What if just one
lifestyle change

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.920
could help you
avoid getting cancer,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.760
or diabetes,
or heart disease,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.200
or high blood pressure?

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.480
This one change could cut
your risk of chronic disease

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.320
and add years to your life.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.080
Well, the simple solution to
so many of these problems

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.400
is to eat a healthy diet.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.760
In other words, one centered
around whole plant foods.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.360
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.840
I'm your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.160
Today, we discover the some of the
health benefits of eating onions.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.160
Onions are potentially a
good source of antioxidants,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.040
though interestingly, the
antioxidants are concentrated

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.880
in the outer layers immediately
under the papery peel.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.240
Unfortunately, most consumers
discard these most nutrient-rich

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.520
outermost layers, thus
losing a valuable part.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.560
Here are some numbers.
Look at that!

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.800
More than 10 times
more antioxidants in the

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.320
outer layer of white onions,
compared to the core.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.560
You’ll also note that
yellow onions in general

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.320
have more antioxidants than white.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.760
And red onions beat
them both, based on three

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.440
different antioxidant testing methods.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.560
That’s why I always try to buy red.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.200
Though red onions are
indeed slightly better, yellow

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.600
or white onions are no
slouches, containing

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.920
considerable levels of
antioxidant activity.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.000
So, nutritious, sure, but
are there any particular

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.240
clinical benefits to onion consumption?

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.840
Evidently, older white women
who consumed onions at least

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.520
once a day had an overall bone
density that was 5% greater than

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.963
individuals who consumed onions
no more than once a month (P &lt; 0.03).

00:01:35.963 --> 00:01:38.320
Now 5% might not sound like
a lot, but that improvement in

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.200
bone density could potentially
translate into decreasing

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.200
their risk of hip fracture
by more than 20%

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.400
if indeed, it was cause and effect.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.200
Daily administration of onion did
cause a big bump in bone density.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.480
This opens the possibility for
a low-cost, safe, and effective

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.120
nutritional approach
to osteoporosis and—

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.360
you guessed it...in the rat.
Another rodent study.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:01.860
Rats!

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.880
But finally, here we go.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.560
Tremendous strides have been made

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:10.000
in treating osteoporosis with
drugs, but they have the potential

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.720
for serious adverse side effects,
so scientists have drawn

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.560
their attention to natural remedies.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.000
So, let’s randomize people
to drink... onion juice

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.640
or placebo onion juice.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.920
I don’t know what sounds worse:
sugary onion juice

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.080
or fake sugary onion juice.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.840
And as if drinking onion
juice wasn’t bad enough,

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.840
it was all for nothing.
It didn’t even work.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.480
What about the antiallergy
activities of shallots

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.880
and any therapeutic effects
on helping allergic runny noses?

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.520
Sixteen such patients were
randomized equally into

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.800
an antihistamine group or a
group that got antihistamines

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.320
plus some capsules containing
dried shallot powder.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.000
And it looked like the
shallot group did better,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.680
but there was actually no
statistically significant difference

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.920
in total symptoms
between the two groups;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.800
so, another #OnionFail.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.480
There has to be something
onions can do.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.840
What about testing the effects of
fresh yellow onion consumption

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.680
on breast cancer patients to try
to decrease the toxic effects

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.240
of a chemotherapy drug
called doxorubicin?

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.640
Unfortunately, no significant
benefit was found on decreasing

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.320
damage to the liver or heart.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.560
But here we go . . . finally,
a clinical benefit to onions.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.360
The consumption of fresh
yellow onion ameliorates

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.160
the high blood sugars and
insulin resistance in breast cancer

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.960
patients during doxorubicin-
based chemotherapy.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.800
Doxorubicin isn’t just toxic
to the liver and heart,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.680
but it may also contribute
to insulin resistance.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.400
So, let’s do a randomized, triple-blind,
controlled clinical trial

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.120
randomizing patients to
like a whole onion a day

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.680
or a third of an onion
a day. What happened?

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:59.440
The high onion group experienced
a significant decrease

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.280
in blood sugars and insulin resistance

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.520
compared to the low onion group.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.160
They went up in the low onion group
but down in the high onion group.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:11.040
So, make onions your friend.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.840
What’s the worst that can happen—
a little onion breath and BO?

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.760
Probably the least of your worries
if you have cancer on chemotherapy.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.640
In our next story,

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:26.400
we learn what an eighth of a
teaspoon a day of onion powder

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.680
can do for body fat,

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:31.200
and what can raw red
onion do for cholesterol.

00:04:32.141 --> 00:04:34.400
Enter "The Effect of Steamed Onion

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.280
Consumption on Body Fat
in Overweight Subjects."

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.160
The researchers used steamed onions
so they could better disguise it

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.600
against placebo, and dried it into
an onion powder, and gave people

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.800
just a minuscule amount, about
an eighth of a teaspoon a day.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.840
Surely, a little daily dusting of onion
powder isn't going to affect the course

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.240
of people's weight, but check out
these results reported in the abstract:

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.000
a significant reduction in body fat
mass on both DEXA scan

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.680
and measured by CT scan —
a significant decrease in the areas

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.240
of whole fat, visceral fat,
and subcutaneous fat.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.240
Hold on. If a little onion powder
is so effective for weight loss,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.880
why wasn't it featured in my
book "How Not to Diet?"

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:18.160
Because, as so often happens
in studies, the spin in the abstract

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.360
doesn't accurately represent
the actual data.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.000
Here are the DEXA scan results.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.040
No significant change of fat in the
group that got the placebo capsules;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.360
they only appeared to lose
about a spoonful of fat.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.440
Whereas the group unknowingly taking
an eighth of a teaspoon of onion powder

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:37.120
stuffed into capsules lost nearly
one and a half pounds of body fat,

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.600
a significant drop from baseline,
but not a statistically significant drop

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:43.600
compared to the placebo group,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.320
meaning the loss could very
well have been due to chance.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.040
Same thing with the CT scan results:
five times more loss of overall fat,

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.840
and 30 times more loss of
the dangerous visceral fat,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.920
but did not reach statistical
significance compared to placebo.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.560
A more recent study tried four
teaspoons of onion powder a day

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:05.520
and similarly failed to
accelerate the loss of body fat

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.960
compared to placebo,
but the placebo was also

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.720
four teaspoons of
onion powder a day.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.040
They used yellow onions versus white
onions, and it seems they both may

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:18.880
have caused a loss of body fat, without
a significant difference between them.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.600
Either way, you can look at these
two studies and say, yeah,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.880
but what are the downsides?

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.880
I mean just an eighth teaspoon of onion
powder a day. Why not give it a try?

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.320
Can't hurt, but we just
don't have confidence

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.760
that it's actually going to help.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.120
Turning now to polycystic ovary syndrome,
also known as P.C.O.S., or PCOS.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:41.360
It's one of the most common hormone
disorders, affecting 5% to 10%

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.920
of reproductive-aged women. In addition
to symptoms like irregular periods,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:50.560
PCOS is also a pre-diabetic state
with decreased insulin sensitivity.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.960
PCOS treatment is a big challenge
due to medication side effects.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:56.960
So what about using dietary options?

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.840
How about a randomized controlled
clinical trial of raw red onion intake?

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:07.520
Why onions? Well, onion extracts
evidently help in diabetic rats,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.160
and more importantly
in diabetic humans,

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:12.720
but evidently not in
non-diabetic humans.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.840
PCOS sufferers are kind of pre-diabetic,
so would it work for them?

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.040
First, let's look at those
other two studies.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.440
Here's the diabetic one on the "Metabolic
Effects of Onion and Green Beans."

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:26.800
Diabetics spent a week eating
either a small onion a day,

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.600
or the same diet with about five
cups of green beans instead,

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:31.440
and they both worked.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.240
The onion group dropped people's
blood sugars by about 10%,

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.160
and the green beans more like 15%.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.760
Here's the study that supposedly shows
no blood sugar benefits for non-diabetics.

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.560
It's true, onions don't lower normal
blood sugars, which is a good thing,

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.000
but check out what happens
when you feed people sugar.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.360
Have people down about two tablespoons
of corn syrup, and their blood sugars

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.080
shoot up over the next two hours before
their body's able to tamp it back down.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.040
But give people the exact same amount
of sugar, but with more and more onion,

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:06.000
and the blood sugar spike
is significantly dampened,

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.440
almost as much as if you had instead
given them an antidiabetic drug.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.480
And we see the same when you
give people a shot of adrenaline —

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.920
with or without onions.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.520
So, are there blood sugar benefits for
diabetics and non-diabetics alike?

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.480
No difference was found between
the higher and lower onion groups

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.600
of PCOS patients, nor
were there any differences

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:32.160
in a marker of inflammation
between the two groups.

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.120
But women with PCOS aren't
just at higher risk for diabetes

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.760
and inflammation,
but also high cholesterol.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.720
Women with PCOS are
seven times more likely

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:43.680
to have a heart attack, the
number one killer of women,

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:47.120
but raw red onion consumption
appears to be effective

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:48.880
as a cholesterol-lowering food,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.680
though the high onion group
only dropped their LDL cholesterol

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.520
about five points, not significantly
different than the low onion group.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.640
I did find this study from nearly 50
years ago where researchers fed

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.080
people nearly an entire stick of
butter, and their cholesterol shot up

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.400
about 30 points within
hours of consumption,

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.240
but only nine points or three points

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.600
when combined with about a
third cup of raw or boiled onion,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.880
the moral of the story being
don't eat a stick of butter.

